Title: The jmacs Manual
Author: J. McKenzie Alexander
Date: 2026-05-22
---

# The jmacs Manual

This is the manual for *jmacs* — a Lisp-extensible editor with an
Electron presentation layer. It explains what the editor is, how it is
put together, how to use it, and how to extend it.

It is a companion to two documents already in `docs/`: `VISION.md` says
*why* the editor exists and what it is for; `ARCHITECTURE.md` describes
the layered structure in design terms. This manual is the practical
account — the one to read when you want to *do* something. Where the
two overlap, this manual links rather than repeats.

The per-function reference lives separately, under `docs/reference/`.
This manual describes the editor as a whole; the reference documents
each Lisp procedure one by one.

---

## 1. What jmacs is

jmacs is a successor in spirit to Emacs, rebuilt on a clean foundation.
Its organising principle is *legibility*: the architecture has a shape
you can hold in your head, the language has rules you can state
precisely, and the system explains itself when you ask. See `VISION.md`
for the full statement of intent.

The defining property: *the editor's behaviour is written in its own
Lisp, and you can change it from inside the editor while it runs.* The
commands you invoke, the keys they are bound to, the modes that
specialise a buffer — none of these are baked into the host. They are
ordinary Lisp definitions in the standard library, and redefining one
takes effect immediately (§7).

The host — the Electron application and the four packages beneath it —
is plumbing. The editor's *character* lives in the Lisp.

## 2. The shape of the system

jmacs is five layers. Each has one responsibility and a stable
interface to the layers it touches. `ARCHITECTURE.md` is the full
account; this is the short version.

| Layer | Package | Responsibility |
|-------|---------|----------------|
| L0 host | `apps/desktop/` | The Electron application — windows, file I/O, IPC. |
| L1 storage | `packages/storage/` | The text data structure. No semantic awareness. |
| L2 buffer | `packages/buffer/` | Text plus a cursor, a selection, editing, modes, change events. |
| L3 lisp | `packages/lisp/` | The custom Lisp — reader, evaluator, macros, modules. |
| L4 renderer | `packages/renderer/` | Projects buffer state into the DOM. Never mutates state. |

Two more pieces sit across the layers:

- `packages/stdlib/` — the Lisp *standard library*. The editor's
  commands, keymap and modes are defined here, in Lisp, on top of L2.
- `apps/desktop/` is also where the layers are wired together into a
  running application.

Dataflow is one-directional. A keystroke becomes a command; the command
edits the L2 buffer; the buffer emits a change event; the renderer
redraws from that event. The renderer never writes to the buffer
directly.

The two interfaces that *compound* — that everything else depends on —
are the L2 buffer API (`docs/api/layer2.md`) and the Lisp itself
(`docs/spec/lisp.md`). Read those two specs before changing anything
beneath the standard library.

## 3. Running the editor

jmacs is a pnpm workspace. There is no bundler and no compilation step.
From the repository root:

```
pnpm install                              # once
pnpm --filter @editor/desktop dev          # launch the editor
pnpm test                                  # run all unit tests
pnpm --filter @editor/desktop smoke        # the end-to-end smoke test
pnpm --filter @editor/desktop screenshot   # capture a PNG of the editor
```

Run the smoke test on its own — it is flaky when it competes with the
unit suite for resources. A pre-commit hook runs the unit suite and
refuses commits straight to `main`.

## 4. Using the editor

### 4.1 The buffer

A *buffer* is a piece of text together with the state an editor needs
to work on it: a cursor, a selection, an undo history, and a mode. The
text on screen is one buffer; the editor holds a list of them with one
*current* (§4.7).

Positions in a buffer are *character offsets*, zero-indexed. A range is
half-open — `[start, end)` includes `start` and excludes `end`. Lines
and columns are zero-indexed too. These conventions hold everywhere,
from L1 up through the Lisp.

### 4.2 Point and mark

The *point* is the cursor — an offset between two characters. The
*mark* is a second, optional position: the anchor of a selection.

Set the mark at the point with `C-SPC` (`set-mark-command`). Once the
mark is set, the *region* is the text between mark and point, and it is
shown highlighted. While the mark is set, ordinary cursor movement
*extends* the region — the point moves, the mark stays. The region
stays active until you clear it with `C-g` (`keyboard-quit`) or make an
edit.

This is a deliberate simplification of Emacs's transient-mark
distinction: in jmacs the region is "sticky" once set. `C-x C-x`
(`exchange-point-and-mark`) swaps the two ends; `C-x h`
(`mark-whole-buffer`) selects everything.

You can also extend a selection without setting the mark first, by
holding Shift while moving (`S-left`, `S-right`, …) — the shifted
movement sets the mark for you.

### 4.3 Moving the cursor

Movement comes in arrow-key and Control-key forms; the Control forms
follow Emacs.

| Move by | Keys |
|---------|------|
| Character | `←` `→` / `C-b` `C-f` |
| Line | `↑` `↓` / `C-p` `C-n` |
| Word | `M-f` `M-b` |
| Sentence | `M-e` `M-a` |
| To line start / end | `Home` `End` / `C-a` `C-e` / `C-←` `C-→` |
| To buffer start / end | `C-↑` `C-↓` / `M-<` `M->` |
| To first non-blank | `M-m` (`back-to-indentation`) |
| By a screenful | `C-v` `M-v` |
| To a line number | `M-g` (`goto-line`) |

`C-l` (`recenter`) scrolls so the cursor's line sits in the middle of
the viewport.

### 4.4 Editing

Typing a printable character inserts it. `Backspace` and `Delete`
remove the character before or after the cursor — or the region, if one
is active. `Enter` inserts a newline and copies the current line's
indentation; `C-o` (`open-line`) opens a blank line below without
moving the cursor onto it; `Tab` inserts two spaces.

`C-t` (`transpose-chars`) swaps the two characters before the cursor.
`M-q` (`fill-paragraph`) re-wraps the paragraph around the cursor to a
72-column fill. `C-x ;` (`comment-line`) comments or uncomments the
current line, using the comment syntax of the buffer's major mode.

### 4.5 The kill ring — cut, copy, paste

jmacs does not use the system clipboard model. *Killed* text — cut or
copied — goes onto the *kill ring*, a list of recent kills, and is
*yanked* (pasted) back from there.

| Action | Key | Command |
|--------|-----|---------|
| Kill the region | `C-w` | `kill-region` |
| Copy the region | `M-w` | `copy-region` |
| Kill to end of line | `C-k` | `kill-line` |
| Kill the next word | `M-d` | `kill-word` |
| Kill the previous word | `M-DEL` | `backward-kill-word` |
| Kill the sentence | `M-k` | `kill-sentence` |
| Yank the most recent kill | `C-y` | `yank` |

The kill ring is ordinary Lisp state — a list held in `kill.lisp`. The
reference (`docs/reference/commands.jmd`) describes each command; the
internals are open to inspection and redefinition like everything else.

### 4.6 Files

| Action | Key | Command |
|--------|-----|---------|
| Open a file | `C-x C-f` | `find-file` |
| Save the buffer | `C-x C-s` | `save-buffer` |

Opening and saving go through the Electron main process over IPC — the
file dialog and the filesystem work happen in L0. Opening a file sets
the buffer's major mode from the file's name (§6).

### 4.7 Working with several buffers

The editor holds a list of buffers with one current.

| Action | Key | Command |
|--------|-----|---------|
| Next / previous buffer | `C-x →` / `C-x ←` | `next-buffer` / `previous-buffer` |
| Switch by name | `C-x b` | `switch-buffer` |
| New empty buffer | `C-x n` | `new-buffer` |

`switch-buffer` prompts in the minibuffer with completion.

### 4.8 Search and replace

`C-s` (`isearch-forward`) and `C-r` (`isearch-backward`) start an
incremental search; the search loop runs in the minibuffer. `M-r`
(`replace-string`) prompts for a target and a replacement and replaces
every occurrence.

### 4.9 Undo

`C-z` (`undo`) reverses the last change; `C-S-z` (`redo`) reapplies it.
Undo is currently per-edit — one keystroke is one undoable step.
Command-level undo grouping is a planned L2 addition.

## 5. Keys

### 5.1 How a key is named

The renderer reports each keystroke as a normalised string. A printable
key is its own character (`"a"`, `" "`); other keys have names
(`"left"`, `"backspace"`, `"enter"`). Modifiers are prefixes:

- `C-` — Control *or* Command (they are equivalent in jmacs)
- `M-` — Option / Alt (the "Meta" key)
- `S-` — Shift

So `M-x` is *Option-X*. Modified keys are resolved through
`event.code`, so they survive macOS's Option-compose — `Option+X`
produces `≈` in `event.key`, but jmacs still sees `M-x`.

### 5.2 Prefix keys

A key can be bound to a *nested keymap* rather than a command. Pressing
it begins a *key sequence*: the next key is looked up in that nested
map. This is how `C-x C-f` works — `C-x` selects the `c-x-keymap`, then
`C-f` within it runs `find-file`. `C-g` aborts a partial sequence.

The standing prefixes are `C-x` (editor and buffer commands), `C-h`
(help — "describe" commands), and, in a Markdown buffer, `C-c` (the
writing commands, §6.3).

### 5.3 M-x — running a command by name

`M-x` (`execute-command`) opens the *command palette*: type a command
name, with completion, and run it. Every command reachable from the
keymap is offered. `M-x` is how you reach commands that have no key
binding, and how you discover the ones that do.

> A current limitation: editor keybindings fire only when the editor
> surface itself has focus — not when the REPL or the minibuffer input
> is focused. Click back into the text to restore key handling.

## 6. Modes

A *mode* is a tagged behavioural configuration for a buffer. A buffer
has exactly one *major mode* — its primary character — and zero or more
*minor modes* — orthogonal, toggleable behaviours. Major and minor
modes are the same kind of value: a plain Lisp map. The full design is
in `docs/spec/modes.md`.

### 6.1 Major modes

The major mode gives a buffer its identity: a display name (shown in
the modeline), a comment syntax, a syntax-highlighting hint, and an
optional keymap. The shipped major modes are `fundamental-mode`,
`lisp-mode`, `markdown-mode`, `javascript-mode`, `html-mode`,
`latex-mode`, `python-mode` and `makefile-mode`.

The major mode is chosen from the buffer's name by a *registry* that
maps filename suffixes to modes (`.lisp` → `lisp-mode`, `.jmd` and
`.md` → `markdown-mode`, and so on). A name that matches nothing gets
`fundamental-mode`.

### 6.2 Minor modes

Minor modes stack on top of the major mode. Each carries an explicit
`:priority`; higher-priority modes are consulted first when a key is
looked up. Enabling or disabling a minor mode runs its `:on-enable` /
`:on-disable` hook.

A key is resolved through a *chain*: the active minor-mode keymaps
(highest priority first), then the major-mode keymap, then the global
keymap. The first map that binds the key wins — so a mode can shadow a
global binding for its own buffers without disturbing any other.

### 6.3 Markdown mode and the math minor mode

`markdown-mode` is the worked example. It binds `C-c` as a prefix for
writing commands — `C-c b` for strong text, `C-c i` for emphasis,
`C-c 1`…`C-c 6` for headings, `C-c l` for a link, `C-c k` for a
citation, and more. The reference documents each. These commands emit
*JMarkdown* syntax — `*strong*`, `/emphasis/`, `==highlight==` — the
dialect used for this project's prose.

`C-c m` toggles `math-mode`, a minor mode in the AUCTeX style: with it
on, a backtick followed by a letter inserts the corresponding LaTeX
symbol (`` ` `` then `a` gives `\alpha`). A backtick followed by an
unmapped key inserts that key, so `` ` `` `` ` `` gives a literal
backtick.

## 7. Extending jmacs

This is the point of the editor. Extension happens in Lisp, against the
buffer primitives and the standard library. `docs/spec/lisp.md` is the
language specification; this section is the orientation.

### 7.1 The REPL

The editor has a Lisp REPL. Expressions evaluated there run against the
*live* editor — the buffer primitives operate on the buffer the view is
displaying, so `(insert! "hello")` in the REPL types into the visible
document. The REPL is how you try things.

### 7.2 Defining a command

A command is just a procedure of no arguments. Define one with
`define`, give it a docstring, and build it from the buffer primitives:

```lisp
(define (insert-divider)
  "Insert a horizontal rule on its own line."
  (insert! "\n---\n"))
```

The docstring is not a comment — the editor keeps it (§7.5). Any
procedure can be run by name with `M-x`.

### 7.3 Binding a key

Keymaps are plain Lisp maps from a key string to either a command
*name* (a symbol) or a nested keymap. Bind a key by putting it in a
keymap:

```lisp
(set! the-keymap (assoc the-keymap "C-d" 'delete-forward))
```

Commands are bound *by name* and resolved late — every time the key is
pressed, the symbol is looked up afresh. This is why redefining a
command takes effect immediately, and why a feature file can bind a key
to a command that does not exist yet.

### 7.4 Hot reload

`C-x C-r` (`reload-stdlib`) re-evaluates the whole standard library.
Because commands resolve by name, the running editor switches to the
new definitions at once — no restart. Edit a command, reload, and the
next keystroke uses the new version. This is the core of the editor's
extensibility loop, and it rests on the Lisp's module hot-reload
semantics (`docs/spec/lisp.md`, §6).

### 7.5 The editor explains itself

Every procedure defined with `define` keeps its docstring and the
source location it was defined at. Three primitives surface that:
`(doc proc)`, `(where-defined proc)` and `(describe proc)`. Two
commands present it interactively:

- `C-h k` (`describe-key`) — press a key; jmacs reports the command it
  runs and that command's docstring.
- `C-h f` (`describe-command`) — prompts for a command name and shows
  its documentation.

"The editor explains itself" is a design principle, not a feature. The
reference under `docs/reference/` is the hand-written companion to that
built-in self-description.

### 7.6 Writing a mode

A major or minor mode is created with `define-mode`, which takes a name
and keyword pairs:

```lisp
(define-mode text-mode
  :name "Text"
  :comment-prefix nil
  :highlight nil)
```

A mode with a `:keymap` refers to it *by symbol name*, so the keymap
stays live-editable after the mode is defined. `register-mode`
associates a filename suffix with a major mode. Minor modes are turned
on and off with `enable-minor-mode` / `disable-minor-mode`. See
`docs/spec/modes.md` for the model and the reference for each
procedure.

### 7.7 JavaScript as a second extension language

Lisp is the primary extension language — it gives the editor its
character — but JavaScript is first-class too, since the runtime is
already JavaScript. The host exposes the L2 buffer to both. Today
interop runs host-to-Lisp (JavaScript functions registered as Lisp
primitives); the Lisp-to-JavaScript direction is planned. See
`docs/spec/lisp.md` §9.

## 8. Where things live

A map of the source, for when the reference points you at a file:

- `apps/desktop/src/app.js` — the renderer entry: buffer primitives,
  the buffer list, the modeline, the splash, the command wiring.
- `apps/desktop/src/main.js` — the Electron main process.
- `packages/renderer/src/view.js` — the editor view: rendering,
  virtualisation, mouse and key handling, the layer stack.
- `packages/renderer/src/highlight.js`, `treesitter.js` — syntax
  highlighting.
- `packages/lisp/src/` — the Lisp: `reader.js`, `eval.js`,
  `primitives.js`, `interpreter.js`, `environment.js`, `values.js`.
- `packages/stdlib/lisp/*.lisp` — the editor's commands, keymap and
  modes, in Lisp.
- `packages/stdlib/src/buffer-primitives.js` — the Lisp↔buffer bridge.
- `packages/buffer/src/buffer.js` — the L2 buffer.

## 9. The reference

The per-function reference lives in `docs/reference/`:

- `index.jmd` — orientation: the tiers of procedures, naming
  conventions, how to read an entry.
- `commands.jmd` — every command in the standard library, grouped by
  task, with key bindings.
- `buffer-primitives.jmd` — the host buffer primitives the standard
  library is built on.
- `lisp-core.jmd` — the core Lisp primitives and the prelude.

For the language itself — its evaluation model, special forms, macros,
modules, errors — read `docs/spec/lisp.md`.

